A program hands a closure over, and each half of the build answers its own.

`spawn` and `post` are what `passAsyncOffload` has always written for a
blocking call on a language that cannot suspend. Declaring them in `sngl:async`
is what lets a program -- and, the reason for it, a *platform package* -- name
the same two operations.

The two ids are answered on different axes and this pins both at once: `spawn`
comes from the language, so both targets here emit Go's `go func(){}()`, while
`post` comes from the platform and the two differ -- fyne queues onto its
driver, gtk4 onto a GLib idle tick. One source, one language, two platforms is
the smallest program that shows the split.

Nothing here is a blocking call, so passAsyncOffload rewrites none of it: what
each target emits is what the program asked for rather than what the compiler
decided it needed.

deny out/go/gtk4/model.go `fyne\.Do` -- post is the platform's answer, not the language's, so the two targets must not share one
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `gtk4rt\.Post` -- likewise from the other side

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import async "sngl:async"

output {
    go {
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
}

ui.window(title="handover") {
    var n = 0

    ui.button(text="go", @click {
        async.spawn(func() {
            async.post(func() {
                n = n + 1
            })
        })
    })
}
